<Base title="Philosophy">
  <PageIntro eyebrow="Philosophy" title="AI-assisted development needs better building blocks.">Dispatch starts from the observation that software work is too varied to be represented well by a fixed automation diagram.</PageIntro>
  <article class="reading">
    <h2>Why workflows do not scale</h2>
    <p>Workflows are attractive because they make a complicated process legible: give an agent a task, run a series of steps, return a result. But engineering work is full of useful interruptions. A test fails for an unrelated reason. A reviewer wants a diagram. The best next step is to inspect production, wait for CI, ask a question, or split the work.</p>
    <p>When these moments are outside the workflow, the workflow becomes a constraint. The workaround is usually a bigger prompt, more hidden state, or an even larger orchestrator.</p>
    <h2>Capabilities compound</h2>
    <p>A capability is a durable thing that can be used in many situations: creating an isolated worktree, sharing a screenshot, leaving a message, running a terminal session, waking an agent later. None of these decides what the work should be. Each makes a larger set of good decisions possible.</p>
    <p>That is why Dispatch favors small, inspectable primitives. They can be combined differently by different projects, teams, and moments of work.</p>
    <h2>The same tools for people and agents</h2>
    <p>Humans should not need a separate control plane to understand agent work. Agents should not need an imaginary version of the repository. Both benefit from visible state, concrete tools, and an honest record of what happened.</p>
    <p>Dispatch is an experiment in making that shared environment practical.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
